  "notes": "The gate (bench/gate.py) passed at the start and was recorded at the end of each micro_peak process (raw/run_*.json) and before the validation. Large bf16/fp16/tf32 GEMMs lose throughput as n grows (bf16 129.8 TFLOP/s at 2048^3 -> 57.9 at 16384^3) without OC events; the validation shows the 4096^3 bf16 GEMM reads 3.57x the A+B bytes from DRAM (operands exceed the 32 MiB L2). mcc__dram_throughput_* read 0 on every validation kernel. [2026-10-22, after the independent T003 review: validation.csv regenerated by bench/parse_ncu_validation.py (duration_ns / sm_hz now unit-normalised; the raw exports are unchanged); hw_constants.json rebuilt by bench/hw_constants_build.py at 5b799a9823d466c67037b947ee7603684a2d98c3 with --b2b results/20261022_E1_peak_backtoback (ceiling = highest rate in either mode; int8 now 330.6 from a back-to-back slice), the median over all bandwidth repeats (250.32 GB/s) beside the best, and sources for the derived FP32 figure. The claim 'large GEMMs are bandwidth-limited' was retracted: the 4096^3 bf16 GEMM drew 158 GB/s of DRAM (61 % of 258) under ncu.]",
